## Deploy

```bash
npm install
npx cdk synth
npx cdk diff
npx cdk deploy --outputs-file cdk-outputs.json
```

Keystone does not run `cdk deploy` for you: creating IAM roles and trust anchors
in an AWS account is your decision to make with credentials you choose, and
reviewing `cdk diff` first is the point.

Then point the local profile at the deployed resources:

```bash
keystone infra cdk sync-profile --profile {{ profile_name }} --outputs ./cdk-outputs.json
keystone test --profile {{ profile_name }}
```

## Verify the device restriction

The trust policy restricts assumption with:

```text
aws:PrincipalTag/x509SAN/URI == {{ device_san_uri }}
```

**The exact condition key must match the attribute mapping Roles Anywhere
actually applies.** The mapping in this stack is `x509SAN` / `URI`, and the tag
key above is what that mapping is expected to produce — but AWS has changed
attribute-mapping details before, and an unmatched condition key in a `Deny`
statement fails closed while an unmatched one in an `Allow` fails open.

After the first deploy, confirm the tag is present:

```bash
keystone credential-process --profile {{ profile_name }} > /dev/null
aws sts get-caller-identity
```

and check the `TagSession` / `AssumeRole` entries in CloudTrail for the
`x509SAN/URI` principal tag. Until you have confirmed it, do not grant this role
permissions you would not grant an unauthenticated caller.
{%- if existing_role_arn %}

## Trust policy for the existing role

This project does not modify `{{ existing_role_arn }}`. Add the following
statements to that role's trust policy, replacing `TRUST_ANCHOR_ARN` with the
`TrustAnchorArn` output and `ACCOUNT_ID` with the deploying account:

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "KeystoneRolesAnywhere",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "Service": "rolesanywhere.amazonaws.com" },
      "Action": ["sts:AssumeRole", "sts:SetSourceIdentity", "sts:TagSession"],
      "Condition": {
        "ArnEquals": { "aws:SourceArn": "TRUST_ANCHOR_ARN" },
        "StringEquals": { "aws:SourceAccount": "ACCOUNT_ID" }
      }
    },
    {
      "Sid": "KeystoneDeviceOnly",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "*" },
      "Action": ["sts:AssumeRole", "sts:SetSourceIdentity", "sts:TagSession"],
      "Condition": {
        "StringNotEquals": {
          "aws:PrincipalTag/x509SAN/URI": "{{ device_san_uri }}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Adding the `Allow` without the `Deny` lets any certificate under the trust anchor
assume the role. With a one-device ephemeral CA that is still only this device,
but it stops being true the moment the anchor issues for anything else.
{%- endif %}

## Revoke this device

{%- if existing_trust_anchor_arn %}
This project shares a trust anchor, so disabling it would revoke every device
under it. Revoke this device by removing its certificate's authorization instead
— narrow the profile, or add a `Deny` on its `x509SAN/URI` tag.
{%- else %}
Disabling the trust anchor stops this device immediately, and because the CA
signed only this one certificate, nothing else is affected:

```bash
aws rolesanywhere disable-trust-anchor --trust-anchor-id <id> --region {{ region }}
```

Already-issued credentials remain valid until they expire (at most
{{ duration_seconds }} seconds).
{%- endif %}
